# Snyk policy — https://docs.snyk.io/scan-fix-and-prevent/prevent/policies/the-.snyk-file
version: v1.25.0

# ── Canonical base (generated from nswds-devops snyk-policy/base.snyk) ─────
# DO NOT EDIT THIS BLOCK IN A CONSUMER REPO. It is regenerated and delivered
# by nswds-devops (.github/workflows/snyk-policy-sync.yml); local edits are
# overwritten on the next fan-out and reported by the weekly canary.
#
# Repo-specific policy goes BELOW the "# repo-specific" marker that closes
# this block, and is preserved verbatim by the sync. That split is the whole
# reason this file is not carried by the ordinary file-sync in
# .github/sync.yml: BetaHuhn/repo-file-sync-action overwrites whole files,
# and several repos carry policy that is genuinely theirs (nswds-email-
# framework's Snyk Code exclude for generated output, nswds-tokens' one
# javascript/PT acceptance, nswds-app's transitive postcss ignores, and
# dtl-sandbox's path-scoped js-yaml and npm-bundled entries).
# See docs/config-single-source-of-truth.md — Mechanism C.
#
# NOTE: never add a '*:lic:*' catch-all. Snyk matches ignore keys as exact
# issue IDs and does not support globs, so a catch-all is dead config that
# reads as protection. Licence findings have to be enumerated, as below.

ignore:
  # The nanoid CWE-835 ignores (SNYK-JS-NANOID-18506894 / -18506897) were
  # removed on 2026-08-31. They are obsolete, not merely expired: with the
  # policy disabled entirely, neither advisory fires any more at the nanoid
  # versions now in the tree (3.3.17 and 3.3.18 under postcss, verified by
  # scanning with --ignore-policy). Snyk has since corrected the affected
  # range, which had been <5.1.16 and wrongly swallowed the patched 3.x line.
  # Do not re-add them speculatively -- if either advisory ever returns, it
  # should be reported and re-triaged on the evidence at that time.

  # ── Vulnerability acceptances ───────────────────────────────────────────
  # undici, as vendored INSIDE the published `npm` package. semantic-release
  # pulls @semantic-release/npm, which depends on the npm CLI, which bundles
  # its own undici -- so `npm update undici` does not touch it and an
  # `overrides` pin does not reach it. There is no local fix.
  #
  # This ACCEPTS A REAL VULNERABILITY; it is not a false positive. What makes
  # it acceptable is the blast radius, not the severity: the path is release
  # tooling that runs in CI against npm's own registry, never an app serving
  # user traffic, and nothing here is shipped -- the published @nswds/*
  # packages carry only their `files: ["dist"]` build output.
  #
  # Verified 2026-09-07: npm 11.19.1 is the newest 11.x and its own lockfile
  # still pins undici 6.28.0, while @semantic-release/npm@13.1.5 requires
  # `npm: ^11.6.2`. default.json blocks npm majors fleet-wide, so no Renovate
  # PR can move it either. ON EXPIRY, re-check whether npm has bumped its
  # vendored copy and DELETE these three entries if it has -- do not extend
  # them reflexively.
  #
  # SCOPING -- these fail CLOSED. The `npm` segment is required, so a
  # genuinely vulnerable undici arriving by any other route is still
  # reported. Verified empirically against agile:
  #   '* > npm > * > undici'                  suppressed all three
  #   '* > bogus-not-a-package > * > undici'  suppressed nothing
  #   '* > npm > node-gyp > undici'           suppressed nothing ON AGILE,
  #                                           whose paths all run through
  #                                           @npmcli/*. Do NOT read that as
  #                                           fleet-wide: 'npm > node-gyp >
  #                                           undici' IS a real path in 11
  #                                           repos, so that scope is narrow,
  #                                           not inert.
  # A bare '*' would suppress undici everywhere and must never be used here.
  #
  # The leading '*' needs a non-empty prefix, which holds because `npm` is a
  # direct dependency of NO repo in the fleet -- it is always reached through
  # semantic-release, @semantic-release/npm or @workspace/semantic-release-
  # config. A twin 'npm > * > undici' entry would be dead config today; add
  # one only if npm ever becomes a direct dependency.
  #
  # WHERE THIS DOES AND DOES NOT LAND -- two separate gaps.
  #
  # 1. REACH. Delivery follows enrolment: this block reaches exactly the repos
  #    keyed in snyk-policy/repos.json, which is the live list. Treat any count
  #    written here as a dated snapshot and read that file for the truth.
  #    The ordinary file-sync in .github/sync.yml deliberately does not carry
  #    .snyk either, so an unenrolled repo receives NOTHING -- not even its
  #    root file.
  #
  #    Snapshot 2026-10-17: all 29 repos in the fleet are enrolled, so every
  #    repo carrying these advisories also carries this acceptance. That is a
  #    snapshot, not an invariant -- a repo added to the fleet later receives
  #    NOTHING until it is keyed in repos.json too, and one that already has a
  #    .snyk needs a migrate directive carrying its fromSha to be converted
  #    safely. Enrolling also wants the repo confirmed as imported into the
  #    Snyk org.
  #
  # 2. DEPTH. This file lands at the repo ROOT (POLICY_PATH in
  #    .github/scripts/snyk-policy.mjs), and Snyk resolves a policy per
  #    manifest DIRECTORY. So in a workspace monorepo, `snyk test
  #    --all-projects` scans every package with NO policy at all -- not merely
  #    the odd finding. Measured 2026-09-08 before the fix: nswds-ui had 15
  #    already-accepted findings open across seven workspaces, and
  #    nswds-email-design 28 across eight.
  #
  #    THE FIX IS `--policy-path=.snyk`, which makes this one file apply to
  #    every project in the run. Both monorepos now pass it in their snyk:check
  #    and snyk:monitor scripts (nswds-ui#178, nswds-email-design#33) and both
  #    are clear of depth-gap findings.
  #
  #    SCOPE OF THAT FIX, so nobody reads more into it: the flag takes effect
  #    in those npm scripts. Verified 2026-09-08 that NO workflow in nswds-ui,
  #    nswds-email-design or nswds-devops invokes either script. What gates a
  #    PR instead is Snyk's SCM integration, and it never runs the CLI -- it
  #    resolves policy per manifest directory on its own, so the flag never
  #    reaches it. Treat the gap as closed for a CLI scan, not for those
  #    checks.
  #
  #    Do not assume a fixed set of those contexts. code/snyk and security/snyk
  #    are required in most enrolled repos, license/snyk in only one
  #    (nswds-email-design), and a few require no Snyk check at all -- so "the
  #    Snyk check will catch it" is not true everywhere. Read the repo's own
  #    ruleset before relying on one.
  #
  #    POLICY: per-workspace .snyk = REJECTED; use --policy-path=.snyk
  #
  #    That line is asserted verbatim by tools/snyk-policy.test.mjs. It is the
  #    machine-checkable part of this note: regex cannot read the intent of the
  #    prose around it, so the declared policy is pinned instead. Reversing the
  #    decision means editing a line that says REJECTED, which shows up in a
  #    review rather than hiding in a reworded paragraph.
  #
  #    Do NOT instead add a .snyk per workspace. That is 8-9 near-duplicates of
  #    this block which the fan-out does not manage at that depth, so they
  #    drift the moment this file changes. Note also that --policy-path
  #    OVERRIDES per-directory files: nswds-ui's apps/web/.snyk is inert while
  #    the flag is set, which is harmless only because its nanoid entries no
  #    longer fire at nanoid 3.3.18.
  SNYK-JS-UNDICI-19635210:
    - '* > npm > * > undici':
        reason: undici vendored inside the npm CLI, reached only via semantic-release; no upstream fix (npm 11.19.1 still ships undici 6.28.0) and absent from every published artifact
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-UNDICI-19635212:
    - '* > npm > * > undici':
        reason: undici vendored inside the npm CLI, reached only via semantic-release; no upstream fix (npm 11.19.1 still ships undici 6.28.0) and absent from every published artifact
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-UNDICI-19635226:
    - '* > npm > * > undici':
        reason: undici vendored inside the npm CLI, reached only via semantic-release; no upstream fix (npm 11.19.1 still ships undici 6.28.0) and absent from every published artifact
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z

  # ── postcss-selector-parser bundled inside the npm CLI (no reachable fix) ─
  # Every npm the fleet resolves bundles postcss-selector-parser 7.1.4 (via
  # @npmcli/arborist > @npmcli/query). Verified 2026-10-14: npm 11.21.0 and
  # 12.2.0, then the latest, both still bundle it. That is below the 7.1.6
  # fix for SNYK-JS-POSTCSSSELECTORPARSER-20510276. The copy is `inBundle`,
  # so neither `npm update` nor `overrides` can move it. The reasoning matches
  # the undici entries above: release tooling reached only through
  # semantic-release, run in CI, never shipped.
  #
  # The scope fails CLOSED. The `npm` segment is required, so this accepts
  # only the bundled copy. Any other copy (one a repo installs itself) is
  # still reported and must be fixed in that repo: by a lockfile refresh, or
  # by an override scoped under its parent (MAINTENANCE.md exceptions
  # register).
  #
  # Never try to reach this copy with a REPO-WIDE override. Snyk's lockfile
  # parser then applies the override to the bundled edge, finds no match, and
  # rejects the lockfile as out of sync. `snyk test --dev` reports nothing at
  # all for that repo.
  #
  # EXPIRY is staggered with the gray-matter entry, away from the 2026-12-31
  # batch. npm has shipped no fix yet, so this one will almost certainly
  # need extending too.
  #
  # ON EXPIRY, DELETE this entry once EVERY enrolled lockfile resolves an npm
  # that bundles postcss-selector-parser >= 7.1.6. A fixed npm release is not
  # enough on its own: each lockfile keeps its older npm until refreshed.
  # Until then, extend it.
  SNYK-JS-POSTCSSSELECTORPARSER-20510276:
    - '* > npm > * > postcss-selector-parser':
        reason: postcss-selector-parser 7.1.4 bundled inside the npm CLI, reached only via semantic-release; not overridable and absent from every published artifact
        expires: 2027-03-31T00:00:00.000Z

  # ── deepmerge Prototype Pollution (no fix) ──────────────────────────────
  # deepmerge@4.3.1 via react-email. No fixed release. Low reachability:
  # react-email deep-merges developer-authored props/styles at render, not
  # attacker-controlled objects. Scoped to the react-email family so a
  # deepmerge arriving any other way is still reported. Clears on a react-email
  # bump that ships a fixed deepmerge.
  SNYK-JS-DEEPMERGE-19964053:
    - '* > react-email > * > deepmerge':
        reason: Prototype pollution in deepmerge@4.3.1 via react-email; no upstream fix, low reachability (developer-authored props, not untrusted input).
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z
    - '* > @react-email/render > * > deepmerge':
        reason: Same deepmerge advisory via @react-email/render. See above.
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z
    - '* > @nswds/email > * > deepmerge':
        reason: Same deepmerge advisory via @nswds/email (depends on react-email). See above.
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z

  # ── http-cache-semantics (no fix) ───────────────────────────────────────
  # http-cache-semantics@4.2.0 via deploy/release tooling only: @pulumi/pulumi
  # (IaC, deploy-time) and @semantic-release/npm (CI release). Not shipped or
  # request-exposed. No fixed release. Two advisory IDs, same package.
  SNYK-JS-HTTPCACHESEMANTICS-19964064:
    - '* > @pulumi/pulumi > * > http-cache-semantics':
        reason: http-cache-semantics@4.2.0 via @pulumi/pulumi; deploy-time IaC, not shipped; no upstream fix.
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z
    - '* > @semantic-release/npm > * > http-cache-semantics':
        reason: http-cache-semantics@4.2.0 via @semantic-release/npm; CI release tooling, not shipped; no upstream fix.
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-HTTPCACHESEMANTICS-19964068:
    - '* > @pulumi/pulumi > * > http-cache-semantics':
        reason: Second http-cache-semantics advisory, same package/route as -19964064. See above.
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z
    - '* > @semantic-release/npm > * > http-cache-semantics':
        reason: Second http-cache-semantics advisory via @semantic-release/npm. See above.
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z

  # ── braces (no fix) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # braces@3.0.3 reached through build/lint tooling only (maizzle, tailwindcss,
  # eslint-config-next, semantic-release), always via micromatch or chokidar.
  # Scoped on the immediate parent so every root path is covered without
  # enumerating each tool. Build-time only, not shipped. No fixed release.
  SNYK-JS-BRACES-19963945:
    - '* > micromatch > braces':
        reason: braces@3.0.3 via micromatch (build/lint tooling); not shipped; no upstream fix.
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z
    - '* > chokidar > braces':
        reason: braces@3.0.3 via chokidar (build/watch tooling); not shipped; no upstream fix.
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z

  # ── uri-js (no fix) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # uri-js@4.4.1 via eslint. Lint tooling, not shipped. No fixed release.
  SNYK-JS-URIJS-19963963:
    - '* > eslint > * > uri-js':
        reason: uri-js@4.4.1 via eslint; lint tooling, not shipped; no upstream fix.
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-URIJS-19963961:
    - '* > eslint > * > uri-js':
        reason: Second uri-js advisory, same package/route as -19963963. See above.
        expires: 2026-12-31T00:00:00.000Z

  # ── gray-matter Arbitrary Code Injection (no fix) ───────────────────────
  # gray-matter@4.0.3 (critical) evals front matter fenced as `---js`. No
  # fixed release exists, and 4.0.3 (2021) is the latest. Reached only through
  # @maizzle/framework 5, which exists only in nswds-email-framework and
  # nswds-email-starter. They stay on Maizzle 5 permanently (MAINTENANCE.md
  # exceptions register), and Maizzle 6 is the release that drops gray-matter.
  #
  # Maizzle calls gray-matter only in parseFrontMatter() on the HTML it
  # renders. Verified 2026-10-14: both repos invoke Maizzle solely via its
  # CLI, `maizzle build` and (as `npm run dev`) `maizzle serve`, and never call
  # render() themselves.
  #
  # The dev server binds all interfaces, but it renders only files found in
  # the configured templatePaths:
  #   - the request URL only selects an existing template;
  #   - its 404 page renders Maizzle's own view;
  #   - it also statically serves every project subdirectory except
  #     node_modules and `build_*` folders: scripts/, templates/, tests/ and
  #     so on, and in nswds-email-framework also docs/, where its production
  #     build is written (dot-directories and root files are not served);
  #   - its websocket JSON-parses any client message and relays it to every
  #     connected client (that is how scroll sync works).
  # So while `npm run dev` runs, anyone on the network can read those project
  # folders, push content into an open preview, or crash the server with a
  # malformed frame. That is a dev-server exposure, not a gray-matter path:
  # nothing a client sends reaches gray-matter. The evaluated input is always
  # developer-authored source, whose author can already run arbitrary build
  # code.
  #
  # Scoped to the direct @maizzle/framework edge (the path `snyk test`
  # reports), so gray-matter arriving any other way is still reported.
  #
  # WHY THE BASE. Only two repos reach this today. It still lives here rather
  # than in their repo-specific tails, because the base is the single source of
  # truth for fleet .snyk policy and an acceptance in a tail is invisible to
  # fleet review. The cost: the scope limits the ROUTE, not the repo. Any
  # future repo that adopts Maizzle 5 inherits this CRITICAL acceptance. If it
  # also passes untrusted HTML to render(), the reasoning above no longer
  # holds. Adopting Maizzle 5 anywhere else therefore requires re-reviewing
  # this entry first. The MAINTENANCE.md exceptions register sanctions
  # Maizzle 5 for these two repos only, so Maizzle 5 anywhere else is drift.
  #
  # EXPIRY is deliberately staggered from the 2026-12-31 batch above. This is
  # a CRITICAL with no fix that will be re-extended for as long as the repos
  # live. Letting it lapse at the same instant as every other acceptance, in
  # the holiday shutdown, would turn the required Snyk checks red for nothing.
  #
  # ON EXPIRY: delete it if both repos are archived (nswds-email-design
  # replaces them). Otherwise re-check three things and extend only if all
  # three hold:
  #   1. gray-matter still has no fixed release;
  #   2. Maizzle is still reached only through the CLI over the repos' own
  #      templates;
  #   3. they are still on Maizzle 5.
  SNYK-JS-GRAYMATTER-20532010:
    - '@maizzle/framework > gray-matter':
        reason: gray-matter@4.0.3 via the Maizzle 5 CLI (build/serve) over developer-authored templates only; no upstream fix; the repos stay on Maizzle 5 by decision.
        expires: 2027-03-31T00:00:00.000Z

  # ── js-beautify Memory Allocation with Excessive Size Value (no fix) ────
  # Availability only (CVSS A:L): Snyk says a crafted input can make the
  # formatter allocate without bound "via an unknown function". It marks every
  # version vulnerable (>=0.0.0) and lists no fix; its only reference is
  # beautifier/js-beautify#2522, a request for a private disclosure channel,
  # not a tracked fix. Verified 2026-10-14 on main of each repo below:
  #
  #   '@maizzle/framework > pretty > js-beautify' (1.15.4)
  #       nswds-email-framework and nswds-email-starter, the gray-matter repos
  #       above. Maizzle 5 reaches `pretty` two ways, and neither repo uses
  #       either: its build runs it only when the merged config (a template's
  #       front matter included) has a truthy `prettify`, and both repos set
  #       `prettify: false` in production, leave it unset in dev, and no
  #       template sets it; and the exported prettify() helper, which neither
  #       repo calls. The build route would only ever format the repos' own
  #       templates.
  #   '* > mjml-core > js-beautify' (2.0.3)
  #       nswds-email-design apps/builder, via its mjml devDependency. mjml is
  #       a test oracle only (unit and e2e tests); mjml-core calls js-beautify
  #       only with `beautify: true`, which defaults to false and no caller
  #       sets. Dev-only, so only `snyk test --dev` reports it.
  #
  # Neither route reaches a deployed runtime or a published package, and the
  # input is always developer-authored.
  #
  # WHAT THE SCOPES DO NOT DO. A scope decides which PROJECTS the entry applies
  # to. Within a project where one path matches, the Snyk CLI suppresses this
  # advisory on every path, including routes not listed here (verified
  # 2026-10-14, CLI 1.1308.0: the Maizzle scope alone also hid a direct
  # js-beautify). So a new js-beautify route in one of these three repos would
  # go unreported. Review this entry before adding js-beautify to any of them
  # by another route, or turning on Maizzle's prettify or mjml's beautify.
  #
  # EXPIRY is staggered with gray-matter, away from the 2026-12-31 batch: the
  # Maizzle route lasts as long as the repos stay on Maizzle 5, so this will
  # be extended too.
  #
  # ON EXPIRY: bump and delete this entry if js-beautify has a fixed release,
  # and drop any scope whose route is gone. Otherwise extend only if all hold:
  #   1. js-beautify still has no fixed release;
  #   2. neither Maizzle repo sets `prettify` (config or front matter) or
  #      calls Maizzle's prettify();
  #   3. nswds-email-design still uses mjml only in tests, without `beautify`;
  #   4. none of the three repos has gained another js-beautify route.
  SNYK-JS-JSBEAUTIFY-20883334:
    - '@maizzle/framework > pretty > js-beautify':
        reason: js-beautify via Maizzle 5's `pretty`, which neither Maizzle repo enables (prettify off) or calls. No upstream fix; availability-only.
        expires: 2027-03-31T00:00:00.000Z
    - '* > mjml-core > js-beautify':
        reason: js-beautify via mjml in nswds-email-design tests only; mjml-core calls it only with beautify:true, which nothing sets. No upstream fix; availability-only.
        expires: 2027-03-31T00:00:00.000Z

  # ── Licence acceptances ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # Weak-copyleft (MPL-2.0 / LGPL-3.0) and permissive (Artistic-2.0) licences
  # on unmodified, transitively-installed build, CI and dev dependencies. They
  # are not modified and not redistributed: the private apps in this fleet are
  # not distributed at all, and every published package declares a narrow
  # `files` allowlist that embeds none of them. The first-party @nswds/*
  # MPL-2.0 findings are our own licence, chosen deliberately.
  #
  # Do NOT reduce this to "the app is private". Six packages here publish to
  # npm -- @nswds/tokens, @nswds/ui, @nswds/app, @nswds/eslint-config,
  # @nswds/metadata and @nswds/prettier-config -- so a fleet-wide
  # non-distribution claim is false. Nor is `files: ["dist"]` the shared
  # shape: only @nswds/tokens uses exactly that. The others allowlist their
  # own entry points instead, which is why the claim above is about the
  # allowlist being narrow rather than about any one spelling of it.
  #
  # Snyk matches ignore keys as EXACT issue IDs -- it does NOT support globs
  # like '*:lic:*', which silently match nothing. Every ID must be listed.
  # Regenerate with:
  #   snyk test --all-projects --dev --ignore-policy --exclude=fixtures,.next

